.TH GXWI-PRINCIPALS-MANAGER 1 "2026-10-06" "Peios"
.SH NAME
gxwi-principals-manager \- see and manage this machine's users and groups on a GXWI desktop
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gxwi-principals-manager
.SH DESCRIPTION
Principals Manager shows the users and groups this machine knows, and lets an
administrator manage the local ones. A local principal is one the machine
holds itself, in lpsd's store; a built-in one, such as SYSTEM or
Administrators, is defined by authd and is the same on every Peios machine.
It is started from the launcher, and takes no arguments.
.PP
Users and Groups, in the bar, switch between the two lists, and Find narrows
either. Picking one shows it in full beside the list: for a user, their IDs,
home, shell, SID, the ways they may sign in, their groups and their claims;
for a group, its description, IDs, SID and members. An administrator also
sees what a local user signs in with and their SSH keys.
.PP
An administrator may make, edit, rename, disable, enable and delete local
users and groups; set a user's password, what they sign in with and the ways
they may sign in; add and remove their SSH keys and claims; and put users in
groups and take them out, including built-in groups whose members are
recorded here, such as Administrators. Each change is a request to lpsd, as
the person, and a refusal is shown in lpsd's words. The machine always keeps
an enabled administrator who can sign in, and a change that would leave none
is refused. Changes to how a user signs in apply from their next sign-in.
.PP
Privileges lists this machine's local policy: one record per user or group,
under the registry key authd reads at every sign-in. Each record may give
privileges, an integrity level, an owner and a default DACL. A user's page
says what they would get in all if they signed in now. Records are made,
changed and deleted in the registry directly, as the person.
.PP
Anyone may see the users and groups. Changing them needs
BUILTIN\eAdministrators, enabled in the person's token; without it, the window
says You may look, but not change anything, and why. The privileges records
are changed by whoever the policy key's permissions let, which as shipped is
Administrators.
.PP
The lists are read again every few seconds, so a change made with
.BR lps (1),
.BR reg (1)
or by another administrator appears by itself; Refresh (F5) reads everything
at once.
.PP
It needs a desktop: run with no GXWI_DISPLAY, it says so and ends.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/apps/dev.peios.gxwi-principals-manager.toml
Its application declaration, which lists it in the launcher.
.TP
.I /run/ident.sock
authd's identity socket, where the users, groups and their details are read.
.TP
.I /run/lpsd/admin.sock
lpsd's admin socket, through which the local store is changed.
.TP
.B Machine\eGeneric\eAuthn\ePolicy
The registry key holding the privileges records.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR lps (1),
.BR lpsd (8),
.BR authd (8).
.PP
Principals Manager, at learn.peios.org.
